data.content:
Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with native-sized integers. Both `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>` work:

```csharp
Span<nint>  a = stackalloc nint[5];
Span<nuint> b = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

On this 64-bit system, `nint.Size` is 8 bytes and `nuint.MaxValue` is `2^64 - 1`. This has been supported since C# 9 / .NET 5 when `nint`/`nuint` were introduced as first-class types — they're valid in any context where other unmanaged value types (like `int`, `long`) are allowed, including `stackalloc`.
